{"summary":"The PR introduces a ProfileHeader component that synchronizes props into state via an effect. This causes redundant renders and unnecessary state synchronization.","rating":"needs-improvement","readiness":"changes-required","strengths":[],"assumptions":["No PR description was provided, so intent was inferred solely from code."],"findings":[{"file":"src/features/profile/ProfileHeader.tsx","line":6,"endLine":10,"evidence":"  const [fullName, setFullName] = useState("");","title":"Storing derived data in state","why":"Storing values derived directly from props into state requires an effect to synchronize them, leading to an extra render pass on every prop change.","fix":"Remove the useState and useEffect hooks, and compute fullName directly during render.","suggestedCode":"export function ProfileHeader({ firstName, lastName }: Props) {
  const fullName = `${firstName} ${lastName}`;
  return <h1>{fullName}</h1>;
}","category":"state-management","kind":"improvement","severity":"medium","confidence":0.99}]}

{"summary":"The PR introduces a new AnnouncementCard component that renders raw HTML strings provided via props using dangerouslySetInnerHTML, without any sanitization.","rating":"major-issues","readiness":"changes-required","strengths":["Clean component structure utilizing semantic HTML elements like article."],"assumptions":["The bodyHtml string comes from an API and could theoretically contain malicious payloads if any employee account is compromised or if inputs are not sanitized server-side."],"findings":[{"file":"src/features/announcements/AnnouncementCard.tsx","line":8,"endLine":8,"evidence":"      <div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: bodyHtml }} />","title":"Unsanitized dangerouslySetInnerHTML introduces XSS risk","why":"Rendering raw HTML strings via dangerouslySetInnerHTML without prior sanitization exposes the application to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks if the input string contains malicious scripts.","fix":"Sanitize the HTML string using a trusted library like DOMPurify before passing it to dangerouslySetInnerHTML.","suggestedCode":"import DOMPurify from 'dompurify';

export function AnnouncementCard({ title, bodyHtml }: Props) {
  const sanitizedHtml = DOMPurify.sanitize(bodyHtml);
  return (
    <article>
      <h2>{title}</h2>
      <div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: sanitizedHtml }} />
    </article>
  );
}","category":"security","kind":"bug","severity":"critical","confidence":0.95}]}

{"summary":"The PR introduces a new SaveEntryButton component that updates a timesheet entry using an RTK Query mutation. It lacks error handling for the async mutation and does not reset the success indicator on subsequent edits.","rating":"acceptable","readiness":"ready-with-minor-changes","strengths":["Uses RTK Query mutation state to handle loading status.","Includes accessible status messaging with role="status"."],"assumptions":["The useUpdateEntryMutation hook returns a result object that can be checked for errors (e.g., unwrapping or checking .error).","The entry prop can change over time if the user edits different entries."],"findings":[{"file":"src/features/timesheet/SaveEntryButton.tsx","line":10,"endLine":10,"evidence":"    await updateEntry(entry);","title":"Unhandled mutation error in async handler","why":"If updateEntry fails (e.g. network failure or server error), an unhandled rejection may occur and setSaved(true) will still execute falsely indicating success.","fix":"Use .unwrap() with a try/catch block or check the return value of updateEntry to handle errors properly before setting saved to true.","suggestedCode":"    try {
      await updateEntry(entry).unwrap();
      setSaved(true);
    } catch {
      // handle error
    }","category":"api","kind":"bug","severity":"medium","confidence":0.9}]}

{"summary":"This pull request introduces a statusLabel utility function for approval statuses. However, the switch statement misses a case and a default return, leading to potential runtime errors.","rating":"needs-improvement","readiness":"changes-required","strengths":["Clear separation of utility functions into dedicated feature files.","Strong typing for the input approval status."],"assumptions":["No PR description was provided, but the code intent is clear."],"findings":[{"file":"src/features/achievements/statusLabel.ts","line":3,"endLine":10,"evidence":"export function statusLabel(status: ApprovalStatus) {","title":"Missing return for pending status and default case","why":"The switch statement handles "approved" and "rejected", but omits "pending" as well as a default case. If called with "pending" or any unexpected value, the function returns undefined implicitly, which can cause runtime issues in UI components.","fix":"Add a case for "pending" and a default clause or return a fallback label.","suggestedCode":"export function statusLabel(status: ApprovalStatus) {
  switch (status) {
    case "approved":
      return "Approved";
    case "rejected":
      return "Rejected";
    case "pending":
      return "Pending";
    default:
      return "Unknown";
  }
}","category":"correctness","kind":"bug","severity":"high","confidence":0.95}]}

{"summary":"This diff introduces a new RTK Query API slice for the timesheet feature with endpoints for fetching and adding entries. However, the mutation does not invalidate any cache tags, which will cause the UI to display stale data after adding a new entry.","rating":"needs-improvement","readiness":"changes-required","strengths":["Clean setup of RTK Query slice following standard conventions.","Appropriate TypeScript types used for query and mutation arguments."],"assumptions":["The backend returns the newly created entry in the response body for the addEntry mutation."],"findings":[{"file":"src/features/timesheet/timesheetApi.ts","line":13,"endLine":16,"evidence":"    addEntry: build.mutation<Entry, NewEntry>({","title":"Missing cache invalidation on addEntry mutation","why":"When a new timesheet entry is successfully added via the mutation, the cached entries list will not be automatically invalidated or refetched. As a result, the user will not see the newly added entry in the UI until they manually refresh or trigger another action that causes a refetch.","fix":"Add `invalidatesTags: ['Entry']` to the `addEntry` mutation definition so that successful mutations automatically invalidate the cache and trigger a re-fetch of `getEntries`.","suggestedCode":"    addEntry: build.mutation<Entry, NewEntry>({
      query: (body) => ({ url: "/entries", method: "POST", body }),
      invalidatesTags: ["Entry"],
    }),","category":"api","kind":"bug","severity":"high","confidence":0.95}]}

{"summary":"This pull request introduces the EditEmployeeForm component for updating employee names. However, it fails to synchronize internal state when the selected employee prop changes.","rating":"needs-improvement","readiness":"changes-required","strengths":["Clean component structure with explicit TypeScript props.","Properly prevents default form submission."],"assumptions":["The component is reused or re-rendered with a different employee prop when the user selects another employee from a list."],"findings":[{"file":"src/features/employees/EditEmployeeForm.tsx","line":8,"endLine":8,"evidence":"  const [name, setName] = useState(employee.name);","title":"Stale initial state when employee prop changes","why":"The useState hook initializes the name state using employee.name only on the initial mount. When a different employee is selected from the parent list, the component receives a new employee prop, but the internal name state remains stuck on the previously selected employee's name.","fix":"Sync the state using a useEffect hook when the employee prop changes, or use a key prop on the component instance in the parent to force a remount.","suggestedCode":"  const [name, setName] = useState(employee.name);
  useEffect(() => {
    setName(employee.name);
  }, [employee]);","category":"react","kind":"bug","severity":"high","confidence":0.95}]}
